1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Section 4

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,200
Producing enough energy to meet our needs has become a serious problem.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,669
Demand is rising rapidly,

4
00:00:09,669 --> 00:00:15,190
because of the world's increasing population and expanding industry.

5
00:00:15,190 --> 00:00:21,617
Burning fossil fuels, like gas, coal and oil, seriously damages the environment

6
00:00:21,617 --> 00:00:24,850
and they'll eventually run out.

7
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:29,605
For a number of years now, scientists have been working out how we can

8
00:00:29,605 --> 00:00:37,230
derive energy from renewable sources, such as the sun and wind, without causing pollution.

9
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:43,989
Today I'll outline marine renewable energy - also called ocean energy - which harnesses

10
00:00:43,989 --> 00:00:47,760
the movement of the oceans.

11
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:53,494
Marine renewable energy can be divided into three main categories:

12
00:00:53,494 --> 00:01:00,330
wave energy, tidal energy and ocean thermal energy conversion,

13
00:01:00,330 --> 00:01:04,460
and I'll say a few words about each one.

14
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:07,186
First, wave energy.

15
00:01:07,186 --> 00:01:14,671
Numerous devices have been invented to harvest wave energy, with names such as Wave Dragon, the Penguin

16
00:01:14,671 --> 00:01:16,202
and Mighty Whale,

17
00:01:16,202 --> 00:01:23,349
and research is going on to try and come up with a really efficient

18
00:01:23,349 --> 00:01:23,860
method.

19
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:29,467
This form of energy has plenty of potential, as the source is constant,

20
00:01:29,467 --> 00:01:33,780
and there's no danger of waves coming to a standstill.

21
00:01:33,780 --> 00:01:41,234
Electricity can be generated using onshore systems, using a reservoir,

22
00:01:41,234 --> 00:01:43,470
or offshore systems.

23
00:01:43,470 --> 00:01:50,175
But the problem with ocean waves is that they're erratic, with the wind making

24
00:01:50,175 --> 00:01:52,570
them travel in every direction.

25
00:01:52,570 --> 00:01:57,622
This adds to the difficulty of creating efficient technology:

26
00:01:57,622 --> 00:02:05,480
ideally all the waves would travel smoothly and regularly along the same straight line.

27
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:12,568
Another drawback is that sand and other sediment on the ocean floor might be

28
00:02:12,568 --> 00:02:14,592
stopped from flowing normally,

29
00:02:14,592 --> 00:02:17,630
which can lead to environmental problems.

30
00:02:17,630 --> 00:02:24,000
The second category of marine energy that I'll mention is tidal energy.

31
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,559
One major advantage of using the tide, rather than waves, as a source of

32
00:02:30,559 --> 00:02:32,901
energy is that it's predictable:

33
00:02:32,901 --> 00:02:39,460
we know the exact times of high and low tides for years to come.

34
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:44,637
For tidal energy to be effective, the difference between high

35
00:02:44,637 --> 00:02:49,815
and low tides needs to be at least five metres,

36
00:02:49,815 --> 00:02:55,510
and this occurs naturally in only about forty places on Earth.

37
00:02:55,510 --> 00:03:02,783
But the right conditions can be created by constructing a tidal lagoon, an area

38
00:03:02,783 --> 00:03:06,420
of sea water separated from the sea.

39
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:12,870
One current plan is to create a tidal lagoon on the coast of Wales.

40
00:03:12,870 --> 00:03:19,683
This will be an area of water within a bay at Swansea, sheltered by

41
00:03:19,683 --> 00:03:21,143
a U-shaped breakwater,

42
00:03:21,143 --> 00:03:24,550
or dam, built out from the coast.

43
00:03:24,550 --> 00:03:28,815
The breakwater will contain sixteen hydro turbines,

44
00:03:28,815 --> 00:03:36,735
and as the tide rises, water rushes through the breakwater, activating the turbines,

45
00:03:36,735 --> 00:03:41,000
which turn a generator to produce electricity.

46
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:46,891
Then, for three hours as the tide goes out, the water is held back

47
00:03:46,891 --> 00:03:52,783
within the break water, increasing the difference in water level, until it's several metres

48
00:03:52,783 --> 00:03:56,570
higher within the lagoon than in the open sea.

49
00:03:56,570 --> 00:04:04,600
Then, in order to release the stored water, gates in the breakwater are opened.

50
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:13,508
It pours powerfully out of the lagoon, driving the turbines in the breakwater in the opposite direction

51
00:04:13,508 --> 00:04:17,700
and again generating thousands of megawatts of electricity.

52
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:25,260
As there are two high tides a day, this lagoon scheme would generate electricity

53
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:33,900
four times a day, every day, for a total of around 14 hours in every 24 -

54
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:38,220
and enough electricity for over 150,000 homes.

55
00:04:38,220 --> 00:04:41,979
This system has quite a lot in its favour:

56
00:04:41,979 --> 00:04:46,573
unlike solar and wind energy it doesn't depend on the weather;

57
00:04:46,573 --> 00:04:50,333
the turbines are operated without the need for fuel,

58
00:04:50,333 --> 00:04:53,674
so it doesn't create any greenhouse gas emissions;

59
00:04:53,674 --> 00:04:56,180
and very little maintenance is needed.

60
00:04:56,180 --> 00:05:01,859
It's estimated that electricity generated in this way will be relatively cheap,

61
00:05:01,859 --> 00:05:08,957
and that manufacturing the components would create more than 2,000 jobs, a big boost

62
00:05:08,957 --> 00:05:10,850
to the local economy.

63
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:17,796
On the other hand, there are fears that lagoons might harm both fish

64
00:05:17,796 --> 00:05:22,071
and birds, for example by disturbing migration patterns,

65
00:05:22,071 --> 00:05:26,880
and causing a build-up of silt, affecting local ecosystems.

66
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:30,364
There are other forms of tidal energy,

67
00:05:30,364 --> 00:05:35,839
but I'll go on to the third category of marine energy:

68
00:05:35,839 --> 00:05:37,830
ocean thermal energy conversion.

69
00:05:37,830 --> 00:05:44,339
This depends on there being a big difference in temperature between surface water

70
00:05:44,339 --> 00:05:49,345
and the water a couple of kilometres below the surface,

71
00:05:49,345 --> 00:05:52,850
and this occurs in tropical coastal areas.

72
00:05:52,850 --> 00:05:59,523
The idea is to bring cold water up to the surface using a submerged

73
00:05:59,523 --> 00:06:00,000
pipe.

74
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,877
The concept dates back to 1881,

75
00:06:04,877 --> 00:06:05,690
when ...
